shape theory.chemistry.nucleus : theory.physics.hadrons, theory.physics.su3, theory.coherence.law1 {
  type: theorem
  layer: 0
  """
// The Nucleus as Anchor Shape.
//
// Protons and neutrons are baryons: 3 quarks consuming the full
// SU(2)-complex, color-neutral (RGB). They are SU(3)-anchored:
// bound to the lattice.
//
// A nucleus of Z protons and N neutrons is an emergent shape:
// Z+N baryons in mutual contact through the strong force (SU(3)
// lattice fluctuations = gluon exchange).
//
// Nuclear stability from Law 1 (Stable Reference):
//   The strong force provides closure between nucleons.
//   The proton's U(1) charge (positive) creates mutual repulsion.
//   Neutrons provide strong-force coupling without U(1) repulsion.
//   Stability requires sufficient neutron-mediated closure to
//   overcome proton repulsion.
//
// The nuclear binding curve:
//   Small nuclei (Z < ~26): adding nucleons increases binding
//     per nucleon (more contact = more closure).
//   Iron peak (Z ~ 26): maximum binding per nucleon.
//     The SU(2)-complex triangle has 3 legs; nuclear geometry
//     optimizes at configurations filling the local contact
//     geometry completely.
//   Large nuclei (Z > ~26): additional nucleons increase
//     repulsion faster than closure. Diminishing returns.
//   Z > ~83 (bismuth): no stable configurations. Repulsion
//     exceeds closure capacity. All configurations eventually
//     dissolve (radioactive decay).
//
// The nuclear charge Z determines chemistry: it sets the
// U(1) coupling strength that electrons reference.
//
// Derives from: theory.physics.hadrons, theory.physics.su3,
//               theory.coherence.law1
  """
}
